# Kernels these modules are known to be correct for.
#
# One build identity per line, exactly as the console reports it:
#
#     uname -r + " " + uname -v
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The module loader compares a "vermagic" string, and
# vermagic carries only the kernel version, SMP, preemption, module-unload
# support and the architecture. It says nothing about the rest of the
# configuration. Two different builds of 4.14.14-funkey can therefore share a
# vermagic exactly while disagreeing about the layout of struct page, of a
# spinlock, or of the mm internals that zram and zsmalloc reach into. insmod
# would accept such a module and the result would be memory corruption rather
# than a clean refusal - the one failure the loader cannot protect anyone from.
#
# So the launcher refuses to load anything into a kernel that is not named here.
# It is a whitelist of builds somebody has actually verified, not a guess.
#
# ADDING YOURS. Send `uname -a`, `/proc/version` and a copy of `/boot/zImage`.
# The zImage is what matters: the kernel's export table can be recovered from it
# and audited against every symbol these modules import, before anything is
# loaded on your hardware. Rebuilding them needs no firmware change. See
# README.md in this directory.
#
# Note this is deliberately not /proc/version verbatim: that line carries the
# username and hostname of whoever built the kernel, which is nobody's business.

